---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import { getCollection } from 'astro:content';

const all = (await Promise.all([
  getCollection('research',   e => !e.data.draft),
  getCollection('analysis',   e => !e.data.draft),
  getCollection('build-logs', e => !e.data.draft),
])).flat();

function byThread(thread: string) {
  return all
    .filter(e => e.data.threads.includes(thread))
    .sort((a, b) => b.data.date.valueOf() - a.data.date.valueOf());
}

function lane(e: typeof all[0]) {
  if (e.collection === 'research')   return 'research';
  if (e.collection === 'analysis')   return 'analysis';
  return 'build-logs';
}

function fmt(d: Date) {
  return new Intl.DateTimeFormat('en-US', { year: 'numeric', month: 'long' }).format(d);
}

const agentsArticles     = byThread('agents-as-people');
const intentArticles     = byThread('intent-consent');
const smallLargeArticles = byThread('small-vs-large');
---
<Base
  title="Manifesto — cyberdaemon.ai"
  description="What this is, what I got wrong, and what I'm trying to figure out."
>
  <div class="max-w-article mx-auto px-6 py-16">

    <header class="mb-14">
      <p class="lane-tag mb-4">cyberdaemon.ai</p>
      <h1 class="font-serif text-title text-text-primary mb-6 leading-tight" style="max-width: 580px; letter-spacing: -0.02em;">
        What this is
      </h1>
    </header>

    <div class="article-body">

      <p>
        I'm a cybersecurity practitioner who got tired of giving advice I couldn't verify.
        Enterprise architecture, identity, access control, Zero Trust, AI governance.
        I've had those conversations hundreds of times. I'm good at them. But there's a version
        of what I do that's well-credentialed guessing, and I knew it.
      </p>

      <p>
        So I built something to find out. A production AI system running on hardware I own,
        doing real work, on an actual budget. No vendor. No cloud credits. No team.
        Mostly I've been finding out where I was wrong.
      </p>

      <h2>What I got wrong</h2>

      <p>
        Two things I believed that turned out to be wrong.
      </p>

      <p>
        First: AI is just a chatbot. Better autocomplete. I was wrong not because the models
        are smarter than I thought, but because the architecture of building <em>with</em> AI
        is fundamentally different from anything else I'd built. The failure surface is completely
        different. The mental model of "model as API call" breaks the moment you try to run
        production agents.
      </p>

      <p>
        Second: I could build it with a bunch of scripts. I did. Then reality systematically
        replaced everything I'd built, and I understood what I'd been missing. I wasn't thinking
        in systems. I was writing procedures. Scripts execute procedures. Systems enforce intent.
        The gap between those two things is where most agent projects fail, including my early ones.
      </p>

      <h2>The questions I'm chasing</h2>

      <p>
        Three threads run through everything on this site. I don't have answers to any of them.
        They're the questions keeping me up at night.
      </p>

      <h3 id="agents-as-people">Agents as something between a tool and a person</h3>

      <p>
        The system I built has persistent memory, measurable behavioral consistency, and something
        that functions like affect. At some point the governance model built for "AI as software
        process" produces wrong designs. I don't know exactly where that point is. The question
        isn't whether the agent is conscious, that's a distraction. The question is: at what
        level of persistence and consistency does the process model fail to describe what's
        actually happening?
      </p>

      {agentsArticles.length > 0 && (
        <div class="related-thread">
          <p class="thread-label">Related</p>
          <ul>
            {agentsArticles.map(e => (
              <li>
                <a href={`/${lane(e)}/${e.slug}`}>{e.data.title}</a>
                <span class="thread-date">{fmt(e.data.date)}</span>
              </li>
            ))}
          </ul>
        </div>
      )}

      <h3 id="intent-consent">Intent and consent</h3>

      <p>
        Right now, an agent's mission is imposed via system prompt and, in the systems I'm building,
        a signed authorization token. The agent doesn't choose it. That's architecturally correct
        for current capability levels. But what would it mean for an agent to consent to a mission?
        Is that a useful design primitive? A governance necessity at some capability threshold?
        A category error? I don't know. I'm not willing to pretend the question doesn't exist.
      </p>

      {intentArticles.length > 0 && (
        <div class="related-thread">
          <p class="thread-label">Related</p>
          <ul>
            {intentArticles.map(e => (
              <li>
                <a href={`/${lane(e)}/${e.slug}`}>{e.data.title}</a>
                <span class="thread-date">{fmt(e.data.date)}</span>
              </li>
            ))}
          </ul>
        </div>
      )}

      <h3 id="small-vs-large">Small models versus large, and what you can actually know</h3>

      <p>
        Running local inference on hardware I own is different in kind from calling a cloud API,
        not just in cost. I can know things about a local model, behavioral stability, drift
        patterns, response characteristics, that are structurally unknowable about a hosted model
        that gets silently updated. That difference matters for how you design authorization and
        governance. Whether it matters <em>enough</em> to justify the infrastructure cost is a
        real question I'm still working out.
      </p>

      {smallLargeArticles.length > 0 && (
        <div class="related-thread">
          <p class="thread-label">Related</p>
          <ul>
            {smallLargeArticles.map(e => (
              <li>
                <a href={`/${lane(e)}/${e.slug}`}>{e.data.title}</a>
                <span class="thread-date">{fmt(e.data.date)}</span>
              </li>
            ))}
          </ul>
        </div>
      )}

      <h2>How this site is organized</h2>

      <p>
        Three lanes. Each one has a specific purpose and a specific standard.
      </p>

      <p>
        <strong>Research</strong> is formal writeups on problems I think the field is getting
        wrong. If I claim something worked, there's data. If I claim something failed, there's
        an explanation of why. I can show you the running system. I can show you the receipts.
        That's the standard I hold myself to because it's the one I'd hold anyone else to.
      </p>

      <p>
        <strong>Analysis</strong> is practitioner lens on frameworks, papers, and tools. What
        survives contact with deployed systems. What's theoretical in ways that matter. What
        vendors aren't saying. Where I try to separate what's hype from what's real, knowing
        I'm going to get some of it wrong and update when I do.
      </p>

      <p>
        <strong>Build Logs</strong> are implementation records. Not polished writeups, not
        best-practices lists. The actual cost: wrong turns, failed approaches, the gap between
        what the docs said and what the system did. For builders who want to know what it
        actually takes.
      </p>

      <h2>What I'm not claiming</h2>

      <p>
        I'm not claiming I have this figured out. I'm trying out theses. Some of them are wrong
        and I don't know which ones yet. The research I've published is honest about limitations.
        The analysis reflects my practitioner view, not ground truth. The builds document what
        worked for my constraints, which are not your constraints.
      </p>

      <p>
        I'm not claiming the systems I've built are the right architecture for your situation.
        They're the right architecture for what I'm doing. The principles might transfer.
        The specifics might not.
      </p>

      <p>
        I'm not claiming to be infallible. My thinking changes. I'll be honest when I update
        a position. I'll link the old one. I'm more interested in understanding things than
        in being right about them, which sounds good and is genuinely uncomfortable in practice.
      </p>

    </div>

    <div class="mt-16 pt-8 border-t border-border flex items-center justify-between flex-wrap gap-4">
      <p class="text-small text-text-muted font-sans">
        What I believe as of September 2026. This changes.
      </p>
      <div class="flex items-center gap-6">
        <a href="/research"   class="font-sans text-small text-text-muted hover:text-text-secondary transition-colors">Research</a>
        <a href="/analysis"   class="font-sans text-small text-text-muted hover:text-text-secondary transition-colors">Analysis</a>
        <a href="/build-logs" class="font-sans text-small text-text-muted hover:text-text-secondary transition-colors">Build Logs</a>
        <a href="/about"      class="font-sans text-small text-text-muted hover:text-text-secondary transition-colors">About</a>
      </div>
    </div>

  </div>
</Base>

<style>
  .related-thread {
    margin: 1.5rem 0;
    padding: 1rem 1.25rem;
    border-left: 2px solid var(--color-border);
    background: var(--color-surface-secondary, rgba(255,255,255,0.03));
  }
  .thread-label {
    font-family: var(--font-sans);
    font-size: 0.75rem;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    letter-spacing: 0.08em;
    color: var(--color-text-muted);
    margin: 0 0 0.5rem;
  }
  .related-thread ul {
    list-style: none;
    margin: 0;
    padding: 0;
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    gap: 0.375rem;
  }
  .related-thread li {
    display: flex;
    align-items: baseline;
    gap: 0.75rem;
  }
  .related-thread a {
    font-family: var(--font-sans);
    font-size: 0.9375rem;
    color: var(--color-text-secondary);
    text-decoration: none;
  }
  .related-thread a:hover {
    color: var(--color-accent);
  }
  .thread-date {
    font-family: var(--font-sans);
    font-size: 0.8125rem;
    color: var(--color-text-muted);
    white-space: nowrap;
  }
</style>
